{
  "document": {
    "name": "hw-en-fanshawe.jpg",
    "kind": "image",
    "pages": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "text": "Rosemary, Red Sage, Endive, Succory, Borrage, wilde Mayjoram of each a good handfull. You must boyle these herbes in water 1/2 an houre, One ounce of Nutmegs, one ounce of ginger, one ounce of liquorice one ounce of carniseeds [?], Red Dock roots 2 ounces, sweet fennell seeds 1/2 an ounce, bruise Your seeds, slice your Nutmegs and ginger and Dock rootes. You must put all these seeds and spices in a Bagg & hang the bag in the drinke. You may drinke of it 6 or 7 dayes. This is an excellent drinke for any Rheumatick Body that is inclined to a Dropsie.\n\nAn Excellent way to make Suppositors.\n\nTake 2 ounces of Gumm Dragon, steepe it in Damaske Rose water a nights, put it into a Morter, then have ready beat of the best Rock Allum, as much as will make the gumm into a Past, beat it well in the Morter, then take it out, rowle it into the fashion of Suppositors, and when they be dry putt them up in a Boxe & keepe them for your use, they will keep seaven years if you doe not use them.\n\nAgainst the biting of a Mad Dogge taught by Sir Kenelm Digby.\n\nCrabs claves [?] taken in June or the beginning of July, & beat to powder, & therof as much as will lye upon a shilling drunke in 1/2 a pinte of warme Milke is a most excellent Receipt for any body that hath bene bitten by a Mad Dog.\n\nOf Medicine for the Gaunders by Experience I have found most excellent.\n\nTake a",
        "method": "vision",
        "decoration_text": [
          "My Lady Bedley.",
          "Ann Hawkone",
          "St R.D. & Hawkins",
          "My Lady Bedley",
          "+",
          "+"
        ],
        "uncertain": [
          "carniseeds",
          "claves"
        ],
